From 02897a6b854ec1dec79292359a7a8480cc9ba76a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:26:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: amdgpu's COMPUTE power profile while the Dolby
 Vision bridge draws, and the GPU clock in the log

The bridge's frames are short bursts of GPU work, one per refresh period, and amdgpu's power
management, which raises the clock on sustained activity, can keep it low while a frame takes most
of its period. The driver's COMPUTE profile is its answer to that load. The pictures do not change:
the same bytes, at another clock.

GPUPOWER (GPUPowerProfile.h), for the GPU that Kodi draws with (its DRM node), on amdgpu only:

- Where the driver has power profiles (pp_power_profile_mode), COMPUTE from the first frame that the
  bridge draws (native output, HDR10 or HDR10+ as Dolby Vision, Dolby Vision converted to HDR10 or
  SDR, HDR mapped to SDR; not a picture kept on the screen or one that stands still) until 3 s
  after the last one, or the end of the video. Which power driver it is is asked without changing
  anything: the profile that is marked is written again, which swsmu takes as nothing to do and
  powerplay refuses outside the level "manual". swsmu (Renoir and later APUs that have profiles,
  Navi and later): COMPUTE is written over the user's preference and the preference that was there
  is written back at the end; the level stays "auto", so the decoder's VIDEO request goes on. Going
  back to "auto" does not undo a swsmu preference (amdgpu_smu.c keeps it, counted per profile):
  writing the earlier one back puts the counts back as they were. powerplay (Raven, Polaris,
  Vega 10/20): "manual" and COMPUTE, then "auto", which applies the driver's own choice again.
- Nothing is touched where someone else chose: a performance level that is not "auto", or (swsmu)
  a preference that is not BOOTUP_DEFAULT. At the end, what was set is undone only if it is still
  so.
- Where the driver has no profiles (Raphael, Phoenix, Rembrandt, Strix), the clock can be held at
  its highest level ("high") while the bridge draws: an option, off by default (more power used).
- A record in /run/hades-gpu-profile, written before any change: a Kodi that ends without undoing
  it (a crash, SIGKILL) has it undone by the next one when the window system starts.
- Fail closed: a write the driver refuses undoes what was done, and nothing is set again until Kodi
  is started again.
- The clock, read once a second while the bridge draws: average_gfxclk_frequency of gpu_metrics
  (its place in each version of the table checked against kgd_pp_interface.h of Linux 7.3), or the
  level marked in pp_dpm_sclk. Its average goes into the line of native output of every 10 s
  ("; GPU clock N MHz on average (...), power profile COMPUTE"), and into a line of its own every
  10 s for a conversion. Also where nothing is set: on Raphael it is what the setting would be
  measured with.
- All reading and writing of these files, which can wait for the SMU firmware, is done by a thread
  of its own that the drawing thread never waits for; it sleeps without a time limit outside a
  session of frames.

advancedsettings.xml: <video><dvgpuprofile>false</dvgpuprofile></video> leaves the profile alone
(on by default, for an A/B measurement), <video><dvgpuforcehigh>true</dvgpuforcehigh></video> holds
the clock at its highest level where there are no profiles (off by default).

Idea and measurement on a Vega 8 by big-fella (dv-engine-vk 0.1.0-9), taken up by YBLOD
(dangerouslaser); written again here for swsmu, whose preference a return to "auto" does not undo.
